/* Original conceptual guides. Each recipe describes a reasoning process, not a universal solution. */
window.TOPICS = {
 'Arrays & Hashing':{icon:'grid',definition:'An array stores an ordered sequence. A hash map associates keys with values; a hash set stores membership. Hashing trades extra memory for fast questions about what you have already seen.',model:'Imagine labeled drawers. Instead of searching the whole room for a value, compute its label and go to its drawer. An array index is a position; a dictionary key is a meaning you choose.',recognize:['Repeated membership or complement queries inside a loop.','Grouping items by an equivalence rule, such as identical character frequencies.','Counting occurrences or remembering the best answer associated with a key.'],recipe:['Name the question you repeatedly ask about the past.','Choose the smallest sufficient key: a value, tuple, prefix sum, or canonical signature.','Decide whether to query before inserting; this prevents accidentally matching an item with itself.','Update the summary and explain why it contains exactly the processed information.'],invariant:'After processing a prefix, the map or set summarizes precisely the facts that later items need about that prefix.',cost:'List indexing is O(1). Dictionary/set lookup is expected O(1), not an absolute worst-case guarantee. Sorting is O(n log n). Tuples used as keys must contain hashable elements.',pitfalls:['A set discards multiplicity; use Counter when counts matter.','Mutable lists cannot be dictionary keys; a frequency tuple can.','Sorting can destroy original index meaning or mutate the caller’s input.'],memory:'Ask the past a question → choose a key → query → update.',compare:'Use sorting when ordered neighbors matter. Use hashing when equality or membership matters. Prefix/suffix summaries handle contributions from both directions.'},
 'Two Pointers':{icon:'arrow',definition:'Two pointers are two positions whose coordinated movement eliminates unnecessary comparisons. They may move toward each other, in the same direction, or at different speeds.',model:'Two bookmarks divide the candidates into regions. Every movement must have a reason: it should discard candidates that are provably useless, not merely make the loop progress.',recognize:['Sorted pair problems and mirrored comparisons.','In-place filtering, partitioning, or merging.','Linked-list midpoint and cycle problems using different speeds.'],recipe:['Choose what each pointer means and whether the boundaries are inclusive.','State which region has already been resolved.','Use order or another monotone property to justify moving one pointer.','Check crossing, equality, and whether the same element may be used twice.'],invariant:'Every discarded region is known not to contain a better or valid remaining answer.',cost:'Usually O(n) when each pointer moves only forward or inward at most n times. Sorting first adds O(n log n). Auxiliary space is often O(1).',pitfalls:['Two nested-looking movements can still total O(n); count total pointer advances.','Do not move the taller container wall without a proof.','Skip duplicates without skipping distinct valid value combinations.'],memory:'What can this comparison safely eliminate?',compare:'A sliding window is a related same-direction technique, but it also maintains a property of every element between the pointers.'},
 'Sliding Window':{icon:'list',definition:'A sliding window maintains information about a contiguous segment as its left and right boundaries move. It avoids recomputing the segment from scratch.',model:'A movable frame crosses the sequence. New elements enter at the right; old elements leave at the left. Your counts, sum, or deque must change with the frame.',recognize:['Longest or shortest contiguous segment satisfying a condition.','Every fixed-size segment needs the same statistic.','Adding and removing one element can update validity efficiently.'],recipe:['Define window validity mathematically before writing the loop.','Expand the right boundary and update its contribution.','Shrink for the right reason: restore validity for longest windows, or minimize an already-valid shortest window.','Record the answer at the moment guaranteed by the invariant.'],invariant:'The maintained counts and statistics correspond to the current boundaries; the chosen shrink rule preserves the candidate answers you still need.',cost:'O(n) when both boundaries advance at most n times and updates are O(1). Character maps use O(alphabet) space. Sliding maximum uses O(k) deque space.',pitfalls:['A set is insufficient when the target has repeated characters.','Negative numbers can break a sum-based monotonic shrink rule.','A stale maximum-frequency trick is valid for some longest-window proofs, not all window problems.'],memory:'Add right → repair or shrink → measure.',compare:'Use prefix sums plus hashing when arbitrary signed sums prevent monotone window movement.'},
 'Stack':{icon:'cards',definition:'A stack processes the most recently stored item first. A monotonic stack additionally keeps its values increasing or decreasing, removing candidates when their answer becomes known.',model:'Think of unfinished obligations. A closing bracket resolves the most recent opening bracket. A warmer day resolves earlier colder days still waiting for an answer.',recognize:['Nested structure, undo operations, or postfix expressions.','Next greater/smaller element, histogram boundaries, or unresolved future comparisons.','A new value permanently makes an older candidate irrelevant.'],recipe:['Decide whether the stack stores values, indices, or richer records.','Write the ordering invariant from bottom to top.','Specify exactly what a pop means: resolved answer or dominated candidate.','Handle remaining items at the end, sometimes with a sentinel.'],invariant:'Every item on the stack is unresolved and still eligible; the stack order encodes the condition for resolving it.',cost:'Push and pop are O(1) amortized. In a monotonic scan, each item enters and leaves once, so total time is O(n), even with an inner while loop.',pitfalls:['Store indices when distance or width is needed.','For subtraction and division, operand order matters.','Choose strict versus non-strict popping carefully for duplicate heights.'],memory:'Push an unresolved question. Pop when the answer arrives.',compare:'A queue is first-in-first-out. A deque supports both ends and is useful when old candidates also expire by age.'},
 'Binary Search':{icon:'search',definition:'Binary search repeatedly halves an ordered search space. The order may be sorted values, or a monotone yes/no predicate over possible answers.',model:'You are locating a boundary between impossible and possible. A midpoint test must tell you which entire half can be discarded.',recognize:['Sorted data with logarithmic-time requirements.','Find the smallest feasible capacity, speed, or threshold.','A rotated array where one half remains sorted.'],recipe:['Define the search interval and the meaning of the answer.','Write the feasibility test separately for answer-search problems.','Choose lower-bound or exact-match updates and prove each one keeps the answer.','Test one item, absent targets, duplicates where allowed, and the final boundary.'],invariant:'The answer, or its insertion boundary, remains inside the maintained search interval.',cost:'O(log n) comparisons for direct search. Answer search costs O(log R × predicate_cost), where R is the numeric search range.',pitfalls:['A predicate must be monotone before binary search is valid.','Do not mix closed [left,right] and half-open [left,right) conventions.','Integer midpoint updates must strictly reduce the interval.'],memory:'Define the boundary. Keep the boundary. Halve the uncertainty.',compare:'Binary search on the answer is useful even when the input array itself is unsorted.'},
 'Linked List':{icon:'branch',definition:'A linked list is a chain of nodes connected by references. Position is discovered by following links; nodes are not stored at directly indexable logical positions.',model:'Hold the next hand before letting go of the current one. Pointer rewiring changes connectivity, so preserving references is more important than moving values.',recognize:['In-place node reordering with constant auxiliary space.','Midpoint, nth-from-end, intersection, or cycle questions.','An ordered structure needs O(1) removal once its node is known.'],recipe:['Draw a three-node example and name references before modifying anything.','Use a dummy node when the head may change.','Save any link you will need after an assignment.','Reconnect boundaries and test empty, singleton, and head/tail cases within the stated constraints.'],invariant:'The already-processed chain is correct, and you still hold a reference to every unprocessed node you need.',cost:'Accessing position i is O(i). Inserting/removing after a known node is O(1). Full traversal is O(n). Recursion adds O(n) call-stack space.',pitfalls:['Node identity and equal node values are different.','Reversing current.next before saving it can lose the rest of the list.','A cycle makes ordinary traversal non-terminating unless you detect it.'],memory:'Save next → rewire → advance.',compare:'A hash map plus a doubly linked list powers LRU: lookup finds the node, links update its recency.'},
 'Trees':{icon:'branch',definition:'A rooted tree organizes nodes into parent-child subtrees without cycles. Binary trees have at most two children. A binary search tree adds ordering constraints; an ordinary binary tree does not.',model:'Each child is a smaller version of the same problem. Decide what information travels downward from ancestors and what answer returns upward from descendants.',recognize:['Hierarchical inputs, nested subproblems, paths, subtree aggregates, or depth levels.','BST ordering can eliminate a branch.','A node combines child results but returns a different, smaller summary to its parent.'],recipe:['Define the helper’s return value in one precise sentence.','Set the empty-subtree result.','Compute child answers, combine the local result, and distinguish local/global optima.','Choose preorder for inherited state, postorder for child aggregates, or BFS for levels.'],invariant:'Each recursive call obeys the same contract for its subtree; ancestor constraints remain valid throughout the call.',cost:'A single traversal is O(n). DFS auxiliary space is O(height), which can be O(n) in a skewed tree. BFS uses O(maximum width).',pitfalls:['Tree height is not always log n.','A diameter or maximum path can turn at a node, but only one branch can extend to its parent.','A BST node must respect every ancestor bound, not just its parent.'],memory:'What do I need from my children, and what can I promise my parent?',compare:'DFS follows one branch deeply. BFS exposes distance layers. Neither is universally better; choose by the information flow.'},
 'Tries':{icon:'branch',definition:'A trie, or prefix tree, stores sequences along edges. Words with the same prefix share nodes, and a terminal marker records where a complete word ends.',model:'A shared route map for words. Walking a prefix gets you to the crossroads from which all matching longer words continue.',recognize:['Repeated prefix queries, autocomplete, or dictionary-guided grid search.','Wildcard characters that branch over possible next letters.','Many strings share beginnings and should be explored together.'],recipe:['Choose a node representation: dictionary children or a fixed alphabet array.','Walk or create one edge per character.','Mark word endings separately from node existence.','For search with branching, carry the current trie node along with the other search state.'],invariant:'The current trie node represents exactly the characters consumed so far.',cost:'Insert and exact/prefix lookup are O(L) for word length L. Storage is O(total stored characters) in the worst case. Wildcards may branch exponentially in their count.',pitfalls:['A prefix is not necessarily a stored word.','Avoid restarting a full dictionary search at every grid path.','Remove or mark found words to prevent duplicate discoveries.'],memory:'One character, one edge; a flag finishes the word.',compare:'A hash set is simpler for exact whole-word membership. A trie adds value when prefixes guide the search.'},
 'Heap / Priority Queue':{icon:'chart',definition:'A priority queue repeatedly exposes the smallest or largest currently available item. A binary heap maintains that extremum without keeping every item fully sorted.',model:'A tournament whose winner stays at the top. Updating the tournament is cheaper than sorting every participant again.',recognize:['Repeated smallest/largest extraction as values arrive or change.','Top k, k-way merging, running medians, or event scheduling.','The next action depends on the best current candidate rather than insertion order.'],recipe:['State what priority should come out first.','For top k, decide which k items to retain and which boundary should be easy to evict.','Push new candidates and remove stale or excess items.','Use tie-breakers when Python would otherwise compare non-orderable objects.'],invariant:'The heap contains the eligible candidates, and its root has the extremal priority among them.',cost:'heapify is O(n); push/pop are O(log n); root access is O(1). Keeping size k gives O(n log k). Python heapq is a min-heap; negation emulates a max-heap.',pitfalls:['The whole heap array is not sorted.','Sorting once does not solve a dynamically changing priority problem.','Lazy-deleted entries must be checked when popped.'],memory:'Keep candidates; expose only the next winner.',compare:'Use quickselect for a one-time rank query. Use a heap when answers must be updated repeatedly.'},
 'Backtracking':{icon:'branch',definition:'Backtracking explores a tree of partial solutions. It makes a choice, explores the consequences, and undoes the choice before trying an alternative.',model:'A maze of decisions with a reusable notebook. Write a tentative choice, walk its branch, then erase that choice before visiting a sibling.',recognize:['Generate all subsets, arrangements, combinations, or valid boards.','Constraints can reject a partial solution before it is complete.','The task asks for all possibilities rather than one numerical optimum.'],recipe:['Define the partial solution and available choices.','Check completion and copy the result when needed.','Prune choices that violate constraints or repeat equivalent siblings.','Choose, recurse, and undo every mutation symmetrically.'],invariant:'On entry and exit, the shared path and constraint sets describe exactly the current recursion branch.',cost:'Often exponential because the output itself is exponential. Include output-copying cost: subsets require O(n·2^n) output space in the worst case.',pitfalls:['Append a copy of a mutable path, not the live path object.','Global visited sets and path-local visited sets solve different problems.','Skipping duplicates at every depth indiscriminately can remove valid solutions.'],memory:'Choose → explore → undo.',compare:'Memoization helps when many branches ask the same subproblem, but caching a generator of all outputs needs care about path context.'},
 'Graphs':{icon:'grid',definition:'A graph is a set of vertices connected by edges. Edges can be directed or undirected, and the graph may contain cycles or disconnected components.',model:'A map of relationships. First decide what a vertex and an edge mean; a grid, word dictionary, or prerequisite list can all encode a graph.',recognize:['Reachability, components, cycle detection, or shortest number of unweighted steps.','Dependencies that must be processed in an order.','Multiple starting points spread at the same time.'],recipe:['Define vertices, edges, direction, and whether weights exist.','Choose DFS for structural reachability, BFS for unweighted shortest paths, or topological sorting for dependencies.','Mark states at the correct time, usually when enqueued for BFS.','Process every component when a single source does not cover the graph.'],invariant:'Visited or processed states have a precise meaning: discovered, currently on the recursion path, or fully completed. Do not mix these meanings.',cost:'Adjacency-list DFS/BFS costs O(V+E) time and O(V+E) graph storage. A grid has V = rows×columns and constant-degree neighbor checks.',pitfalls:['In undirected DFS, the parent edge is not a cycle by itself.','A directed cycle needs a recursion-path state or indegree reasoning.','BFS distance increments per layer, not per popped vertex.'],memory:'Model the relationships before choosing the traversal.',compare:'Disjoint-set union tracks connectivity under edge additions, but does not reconstruct paths or solve directed reachability.'},
 'Advanced Graphs':{icon:'branch',definition:'Advanced graph methods add weighted optimality, global connectivity, or edge-usage constraints: shortest paths, minimum spanning trees, bounded paths, and Eulerian trails.',model:'Different map questions need different promises. The cheapest route from a source is not the cheapest network connecting everybody, and visiting every edge is not visiting every vertex.',recognize:['Non-negative weighted shortest paths → Dijkstra.','Connect every vertex with minimum total edge cost → minimum spanning tree.','Use every directed edge once → Eulerian trail; cap edge count → layered relaxation.'],recipe:['Write the objective and constraints on the path or network.','Identify the theorem that justifies finalizing or selecting a candidate.','Choose the matching frontier: heap, sorted edges with union-find, or separate distance layers.','Handle unreachable vertices and invalid prefix/order constraints explicitly.'],invariant:'Every finalized decision is justified by the algorithm’s assumptions: non-negative weights for Dijkstra, cut safety for MST, or edge conservation for an Eulerian trail.',cost:'Heap Dijkstra is typically O((V+E) log V). Kruskal is O(E log E). Bellman-Ford is O(VE); a k-edge bound can use O(kE).',pitfalls:['Ordinary Dijkstra is invalid with negative weights.','One in-place relaxation layer can accidentally use too many flight edges.','For a bottleneck path, combine cost with max rather than addition.'],memory:'Route, network, or trail? Name the objective first.',compare:'BFS is Dijkstra’s simpler unweighted cousin. Topological DP can solve shortest paths in a DAG even with negative edges.'},
 '1-D Dynamic Programming':{icon:'list',definition:'Dynamic programming solves repeated subproblems once. A one-dimensional state means one changing quantity—often an index, prefix length, or amount—fully determines the subproblem.',model:'Fill a notebook of answers to smaller questions. The notebook is useful only if each page’s question is defined precisely enough to stand on its own.',recognize:['A best/count/possible answer depends on smaller prefixes or suffixes.','Different decision paths reach the same remaining index or amount.','Only a few recent states remain necessary after evaluation.'],recipe:['Define f(i) in words before coding.','Enumerate the first or last legal choice and combine child answers with min, max, sum, or OR.','Set base cases and cache repeated states.','Choose dependency order for tabulation; compress only when discarded states are never read again.'],invariant:'Every stored entry answers exactly the defined subproblem, with all of its dependencies already correct.',cost:'Time = number of states × work per state. Space includes the cache, input-independent preprocessing, and recursion stack. Numeric target bounds are pseudo-polynomial.',pitfalls:['A state that omits necessary history can merge unequal subproblems.','Do not force a rolling two-value solution when transitions reach arbitrarily far back.','Count, optimize, and feasibility problems use different combination operators.'],memory:'State → choice → base → order → live values.',compare:'Greedy needs proof that one local choice is sufficient. DP keeps alternatives when that proof does not exist.'},
 '2-D Dynamic Programming':{icon:'grid',definition:'Two-dimensional DP uses two independent coordinates to define the remaining problem, such as two string positions, a grid cell, or an item index and capacity.',model:'A table is a map of questions, not merely a grid of numbers. Name both axes, then draw arrows from each answer to the answers it needs.',recognize:['Comparing two sequences while preserving order.','Choosing items under a budget or counting paths through a grid.','Intervals or constraints that require more than one index.'],recipe:['Define the meaning and range of each axis.','Draw the dependency arrows and base row/column or empty interval.','Memoize the recurrence, then choose an order compatible with the arrows.','For row compression, distinguish old-row, current-row, and saved-diagonal values.'],invariant:'The cell being evaluated reads the correct version of every dependency—previous layer or current layer as required.',cost:'Often O(mn) states, but transitions can add another factor. Interval DP with all split points is commonly O(n³) time and O(n²) space.',pitfalls:['Ascending versus descending inner loops can change 0/1 choices into unlimited reuse.','Substring mismatches reset; subsequence mismatches branch.','An entire interval table generally cannot be compressed to two ordinary rows.'],memory:'Name the axes; follow the arrows.',compare:'The visible table dimensions do not automatically equal state dimensions; synchronized robots need a shared row plus two columns.'},
 'Greedy':{icon:'arrow',definition:'A greedy algorithm commits to a locally chosen option without revisiting it. Correctness requires showing that some optimal solution agrees with that choice.',model:'You are allowed to cross out alternatives only with a proof. Exchange arguments show that replacing an optimal solution’s choice with yours cannot make it worse.',recognize:['An earliest finish, furthest reach, or smallest remaining item forces a safe choice.','A failed segment can rule out all starts inside it.','The problem permits a maintained bound or range instead of enumerating every history.'],recipe:['Propose the local choice and explain why it is safe.','Try to construct a small counterexample before implementing it.','Use an exchange argument, dominance, or staying-ahead proof.','State what progress the maintained boundary or resource balance guarantees.'],invariant:'After every commitment, at least one optimal solution remains consistent with all commitments made so far.',cost:'Often O(n), or O(n log n) when sorting exposes safe choices. A short implementation is not evidence of correctness.',pitfalls:['Greedy coin selection fails for arbitrary denominations.','A necessary total-resource condition may not identify a valid start by itself.','Do not reuse a greedy shortcut after adding fees, limits, or cooldowns without re-proving it.'],memory:'Choose locally only after proving globally.',compare:'When a local choice is not provably safe, retain alternatives with DP, search, or a priority queue.'},
 'Intervals':{icon:'list',definition:'An interval describes a range between two endpoints. Problems combine geometric overlap with ordering, selection, resource usage, or queries.',model:'Lay meetings or segments on a number line. Most confusion disappears once you mark whether endpoints are included and what touching means.',recognize:['Merge coverage, schedule non-overlapping events, or count simultaneous activity.','Insert into an already disjoint sorted list.','Answer many point-in-interval queries offline.'],recipe:['Decide whether intervals are closed or half-open.','Choose the sorting key that serves the objective: start for merging, end for selection.','Maintain only the relevant frontier or active candidates.','Specify tie ordering at shared endpoints and restore original query order if needed.'],invariant:'The processed prefix is settled, and the active set contains exactly intervals that can still affect future positions.',cost:'Sorting usually dominates at O(n log n). A scan after sorting is O(n); heaps add O(log n) per activation/removal.',pitfalls:['Touching closed intervals overlap for coverage but half-open meetings can share an endpoint.','Sorting by start is not the same greedy proof as selecting by earliest end.','For integer closed intervals, length is right−left+1.'],memory:'Draw the endpoints before choosing the inequality.',compare:'Merging finds coverage; scheduling chooses intervals; room counting measures peak overlap. They are different objectives.'},
 'Math & Geometry':{icon:'grid',definition:'Mathematical and geometric problems use numerical structure, coordinate transformations, digit operations, or counting identities to avoid unnecessary search.',model:'Choose a representation in which the operation becomes local: digit plus carry, coordinate permutation, boundary movement, or repeated squaring.',recognize:['Matrix rotation or traversal, decimal arithmetic, repeated deterministic number transformations.','An exponent can be decomposed into binary digits.','A geometric shape fixes missing coordinates once enough corners are chosen.'],recipe:['Write the mathematical transformation independently of code.','Check sign, base, precision, and coordinate conventions.','Translate the formula into a loop with a clear carry or boundary invariant.','Test symmetry, zero, one row/column, and overflow where specified.'],invariant:'The processed portion is mathematically correct, and the remaining carry, exponent, or boundaries exactly encode unfinished work.',cost:'Count digit operations and matrix cells, not just lines of code. Binary exponentiation uses O(log |n|) multiplications. Grade-school multiplication uses O(mn) digit work.',pitfalls:['Python // floors negative values; truncation toward zero is different.','Floating-point shortcuts can lose integer accuracy.','In-place matrix markers must distinguish original zeros from newly written zeros.'],memory:'Change the representation until the rule becomes simple.',compare:'An elegant formula must respect the problem’s constraints; converting a whole numeric string to int can violate the intended task.'},
 'Bit Manipulation':{icon:'code',definition:'Bit manipulation treats an integer as a sequence of binary digits. AND, OR, XOR, shifts, and masks operate on those digits directly.',model:'A row of switches. A mask chooses switches; XOR toggles them; shifting moves them into different place values.',recognize:['Pair cancellation, powers of two, subsets encoded as masks, or fixed-width arithmetic.','Repeatedly remove one set bit.','A numeric recurrence relates a number to itself with its last bit removed.'],recipe:['Fix the width and whether values are signed.','Write a tiny binary example before using an identity.','Name what each mask isolates or clears.','For Python signed simulation, mask intermediate results and explicitly interpret the sign bit.'],invariant:'Each processed bit contributes to the intended place, and any unprocessed carry or mask remains inside the stated width.',cost:'For fixed 32-bit inputs many operations are O(1). For unbounded Python integers, costs depend on the number of machine words; do not blindly assume constant time.',pitfalls:['~x in Python uses unbounded signed semantics, not a 32-bit complement.','XOR is not ordinary addition because it omits carries.','Reverse exactly the required number of bits, including leading zeros.'],memory:'XOR cancels; AND selects; shifts place; masks bound.',compare:'Bit tricks are compact representations of a proof. Explain the identity before memorizing its syntax.'}
};
// The original Striver categories share these foundations and add their own specialized rules.
Object.assign(window.TOPICS,{
 '1D DP':{...window.TOPICS['1-D Dynamic Programming']},
 'Grid DP':{...window.TOPICS['2-D Dynamic Programming'],definition:'Grid DP defines an answer at a cell or synchronized collection of positions. Legal movement determines the dependency graph.',memory:'Coordinates define the question; legal moves define the arrows.'},
 'Subsequences':{...window.TOPICS['2-D Dynamic Programming'],definition:'A subsequence retains relative order while allowing omissions. Item-and-budget DP asks whether to pick, skip, count, or optimize a selection under a constraint.',memory:'Pick or skip; does picking consume the item?',pitfalls:['Descending capacity enforces 0/1 usage; ascending capacity permits reuse.','Zeros double subset counts even after the target reaches zero.','A boolean table cannot substitute for a counting table.']},
 'Strings':{...window.TOPICS['2-D Dynamic Programming'],definition:'String DP compares prefixes, suffixes, or intervals. Matching, editing, counting, and palindromes differ in what each state promises.',memory:'Contiguous means reset; subsequence means skip; edit means pay.',pitfalls:['Substring and subsequence recurrences differ on mismatches.','Count matching subsequences with addition, not maximum.','Reconstructing a string may require more memory than computing its length.']},
 'Stocks':{...window.TOPICS['1-D Dynamic Programming'],definition:'Stock DP models a sequence of legal decisions. State includes time, whether a share is held, and any transaction allowance or waiting restriction.',memory:'Day + inventory + remaining restrictions.',pitfalls:['Charge a fee once per completed transaction.','A cooldown changes the next day you may buy.','Unlimited positive-difference greediness fails after adding constraints.']},
 'LIS':{...window.TOPICS['1-D Dynamic Programming'],definition:'Longest-increasing-subsequence methods build ordered chains. Endpoint DP asks for the best chain ending here; patience sorting keeps the smallest possible tail for every length.',memory:'Smaller tail, more future possibilities.',pitfalls:['tails is not necessarily an actual subsequence.','Strict increase uses bisect_left, not bisect_right.','Counting or reconstructing chains needs more information than the length alone.']},
 'Partition DP':{...window.TOPICS['2-D Dynamic Programming'],definition:'Partition DP chooses where to split an interval or where the next block ends. The choice must leave independent subproblems with fixed boundaries.',memory:'Try a split. If first entangles, choose last.',pitfalls:['Burst Balloons becomes independent when choosing the last burst, not the first.','Matrix dimensions and matrix indices differ by one.','All possible split points can require the full O(n²) interval table.']},
 'Rectangles':{...window.TOPICS['2-D Dynamic Programming'],definition:'Rectangle and square problems summarize geometric support. Histogram heights encode consecutive ones; square DP measures the largest square ending at each cell.',memory:'Rectangles need heights and widths; squares need three supporting neighbors.',pitfalls:['Sum square side lengths to count all squares; square a maximum side to get maximum area.','A histogram uses a monotonic stack, not the three-neighbor square recurrence.','Reset heights and square values on zero cells.']}
});
